# Pattern matching

Prefer pattern matching (match / switch expressions) over `switch` statements and `if`/`else if` chains when dispatching on a value or shape. Apply in all dialects (C#, TypeScript, Rust).

- Use a switch/match **expression** that returns a value (or `Task`) instead of a statement `switch` with `break`/`case`.
- Bind nested data in the pattern (`{ Create: { } create }`, enum variants, discriminated unions) rather than checking fields then assigning.
- Keep a `_` / default arm that throws for impossible or empty cases.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD
switch (step)
{
    case { Create: { } create }:
        await repo.Add(Row(create.Row));
        break;
    default:
        throw new InvalidOperationException($"{spec.Name}: empty step");
}

// ✅ GOOD
await (step switch
{
    { Create: { } create } => repo.Add(Row(create.Row)),
    _ => throw new InvalidOperationException($"{spec.Name}: empty step")
});
```

```rust
// ❌ BAD — statement-style branching
if let Some(create) = &step.create { ... } else if ...

// ✅ GOOD
match step {
    Step::Create(create) => repo.add(row(&create.row)).await,
    _ => anyhow::bail!("{}: empty step", spec.name),
}
```

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
switch (step.kind) {
  case "create":
    await repo.add(row(step.row));
    break;
  default:
    throw new Error(`${spec.name}: empty step`);
}

// ✅ GOOD
await ({
  create: () => repo.add(row(step.row)),
}[step.kind] ?? (() => { throw new Error(`${spec.name}: empty step`); }))();
```
